    array.push(cH, "The euro got a little breathing room, but the dollar is still the one setting the pace.|Sterling's big exam is this morning, and the dollar walks in with top marks already.|Tokyo cleared its throat as the pair crept toward 160.|A quiet night for the franc, another firm one for the dollar.|Oil slipped again in Asia, and the loonie had nowhere to hide.|The Aussie slid to a seven-month low while the dollar kept collecting hike bets.|No news from Wellington, and that suits the dollar side just fine.|Both sides are waiting for news, and one of them gets it this morning.|This pair can now move both ways in our math, and that makes it one to watch.|The euro's French weight got lighter, and this thin bearish lean could disappear.|A softer French stress meter puts this knife-edge pair at risk of going flat.|Still a stand-off, with the euro inching up from the floor.|Two struggling currencies, but the euro's struggle may be getting lighter.|The pound waits for its GDP verdict while Tokyo warns the market again.|One number this morning could decide whether this thin lean survives.|Two currencies with fading rate stories, and only one of them faces a test today.|A stand-off where one player gets a turn this morning.|Oil kept sliding, giving this slim lean a quiet tailwind.|Still level on the published numbers, but Tokyo keeps adding to the yen's side.|Nothing new on either side, so the stand-off continues.|The Aussie keeps a thin edge over its neighbour on a quiet night.|Oil's slide keeps the commodity cousins apart.|A silent kiwi meets a yen that Tokyo keeps defending.|Two quiet currencies, one thin lean.|The kiwi keeps its thin lead because oil keeps falling.|The weakest currency on our board meets a yen with officials on guard.|Oil down again, franc unchanged: the lean holds.|Two safe havens, and Tokyo just raised its voice.|Gold dipped below $4,000, and the reasons behind it haven't gone away.")
